I'm facing really strange dplyr::summarise behavior, and I can't tell whether it's something intentional that I don't understand, or a bug. Here's the data I'm working with, and a brief description.

> library(dplyr)
>
> info <- read.delim("http://jofrhwld.github.io/assets/audio_stats.txt")
> glimpse(info)
> # Variables:
> # $ transcribed (dbl) 2810.733, 2813.321, 3706.089, 2777.893, 2739.913, 1200.568, 483.747, 904....
> # $ total       (dbl) 2814.345, 2826.450, 3717.926, 3957.084, 2758.806, 2587.360, 492.168, 3799...
> # $ nvowels     (int) 3078, 3702, 5885, 3884, 4119, 1471, 258, 859, 3213, 1396, 836, 2565, 1585...

> sum(info$total)/3600
> # 257.3137
>
> sum(info$transcribed)/3600
> # 198.201
>
> sum(info$nvowels)
> # 629069

I'm running into the summarise error here:

> info %>% summarise(
>     Total = sum(total)/3600,
>     Transcribed = sum(transcribed)/3600,
>     Vowels = formatC(sum(nvowels), big.mark = ",")
>   )
> #      Total Transcribed Vowels
> # 1 257.3137   0.7807592  3,078

That's totally wrong, but I have no idea why. If I drop out the first column, the Transcribed number is correct.

> info %>% summarise(
>     #Total = sum(total)/3600,
>     Transcribed = sum(transcribed)/3600,
>     Vowels = formatC(sum(nvowels), big.mark = ",")
>   )
> #   Transcribed Vowels
> # 1     198.201  3,078

And if I drop out the Transcribed column also, the Vowels number is correct.

> info %>% summarise(
>     #Total = sum(total)/3600,
>     #Transcribed = sum(transcribed)/3600,
>     Vowels = formatC(sum(nvowels), big.mark = ",")
>   )
> #    Vowels
> # 1 629,069

And for some reason, if I drop the formatC from the original call, the Vowels column is now correct.

> info %>% summarise(
>     Total = sum(total)/3600,
>     Transcribed = sum(transcribed)/3600,
>     Vowels = sum(nvowels)
>   )
> #      Total Transcribed Vowels
