As much as I appreciate these wonderful libraries Hadley Wickham has written, I find myself very frustrated with the documentation for reshape2. Is there any document for reshape2 that is comparable to the JSS article on its predecessor reshape (http://www.jstatsoft.org/v21/i12/paper)?

Also, does anyone know where to find some good, *thorough* documentation for acast and dcast? I have found the doc page that comes with the reshape2 package to be so perfunctory and ambiguous as to be nearly useless; furthermore, the description for the drop parameter is inconsistent with the results I get as I've experimented with acast.

From u9a0033d9324fcea3 at groups.google.com  Wed May 28 00:03:05 2014
From: u9a0033d9324fcea3 at groups.google.com (Jennifer Bryan)
Date: Wed, 28 May 2014 00:03:05 +0000
Subject: dplyr::group_by() gives error for character variable protected with I() at creation
Message-ID: <00TBmrhiRV4.3XTmFkHzHFUJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/manipulatr/c/00TBmrhiRV4/m/3XTmFkHzHFUJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

When I want to selectively suppress the character-to-factor conversion for a single variable in a call to data.frame(), I protect it with I().

That creates a variable with class "AsIs" but mode and typeof "character".

This seems to make it impossible to use dplyr::group_by() with the resulting data.frame.

Here's a small illustration of the problem and various workarounds I don't like, such as using stringsAsFactors = FALSE to suppress all conversion and post hoc rescue of a character variable with as.character().

https://gist.github.com/jennybc/7722cb734d9c650f9f85

I can't find evidence that anyone else has struggled with this, so maybe someone can suggest a better workflow? Or should dplyr be OK with this?

Thanks, Jenny

